                <pb facs="0249" n="135" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg135"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">462.00R296/4270: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Edge</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-07-04T02:00:00+01:00">July 4, 1931—2
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 3—10:20 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>402. The following communiqué was issued at the close of this evening’s meeting
                    with the French negotiators: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Mr. Mellon, United States Secretary of the Treasury, and Mr. Walter
                            Edge, Ambassador of the United States, conferred at the Ministry of the
                            Interior tonight with Monsieur Aristide Briand, Minister for Foreign
                            Affairs; Monsieur Pierre Etienne Flandin, Minister of Finance, and
                            Monsieur André François-Poncet, Under Secretary for National Economy.
                            The President of the Council, whose presence was required at the
                            Chamber, left immediately after the opening of the conference.</p>
                        <p>The two delegations continued to examine President Hoover’s proposal and
                            the French reply. Mr. Mellon confirmed the fact that the American
                            Government had accepted the principle of the continuity of unconditional
                            payments by Germany under the Young Plan. Several other points over
                            which there had been certain differences of opinion were eliminated.
                            Those which remain of lesser importance will be submitted to the
                            examination of the Council of Ministers tomorrow.</p>
                        <p>An agreement on the technical and financial aspects of the negotiations
                            seems possible of acceptance very soon conditional of course on the
                            adherence of the other interested powers”.</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Edge</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
